## Decision: DO NOT FLIP DEFAULT-ON AT e8+

### Photos (10 + 3 = 13 cells): 13/13 byte-identical at d=1.0 e8

All photos including the original chunk-6 false-positive cluster are now
byte-identical — the chunk-6 bbox-span gate cleanly rejects every
sub-image ridge segment.

### Synthetics (5 cells): 3/5 REGRESS, 2/5 byte-identical

| Image                          | off    | on    | delta   | pct    | gate outcome |
|--------------------------------|--------|-------|---------|--------|--------------|
| realistic_1wire_1024x256       | 6831   | 7043  | +212    | +3.10% | ADMIT (bbox span 1024 = long_dim 1024) |
| realistic_2wires_1024x256      | 6856   | 7148  | +292    | +4.26% | ADMIT (×2)   |
| realistic_4wires_1024x512      | 13162  | 13882 | +720    | +5.47% | ADMIT (×4)   |
| realistic_4wires_2048x512      | 23654  | 23654 |    +0   |  +0%   | REJECT (×16; polyline tracer caps at ~1042 px) |
| realistic_8wires_2048x1024     | 43861  | 43861 |    +0   |  +0%   | REJECT (×32; polyline tracer caps at ~1042 px) |

The 1024-wide cells admit at the gate (`bbox_span ≥ image_long_dim`) and
then the spline encode actively HURTS bytes at e8: +3-5% bigger.

This is a stronger negative result than `6c01965`'s "zero benefit"
finding. With chunks 5+6 in place, the chunk-6 span gate works on
2048+-wide ridges (the polyline tracer caps at `MAX_POLYLINE_LENGTH ≈
1042` so no candidate clears `1.0 × 2048` span) but is a no-op at
1024-wide ridges where every candidate clears `1.0 × 1024 = 1024`
exactly. At e8 the post-splines XYB feeds the butteraugli loop a
different field and the encoder emits a strictly worse bitstream.

The trial-encode L2-energy proxy (`bytes_saved_proxy = e_drop / 5.0`)
predicts +212 to +303 bytes saved on these candidates; the actual
delta is +212 to +720 LARGER. The proxy is structurally wrong at e8
because it ignores the buttloop's re-quantization of the spline-free
residual on the post-splines XYB.

### Default decision: `auto_splines_default(_) = false` (unchanged)

Flipping default-on at e8+ would:
- Net 13 byte-identical photos (zero gain).
- Net 3 wire-image regressions (+3.1% to +5.5%) on exactly the content
  the detector was designed to win on.
- Net 2 wire-image byte-identical at long_dim ≥ 2048 (span gate rejects).
- Add per-encode CPU: Sobel, NMS, Hessian, polyline trace, trial encode.

The flag stays opt-in. Callers with extreme single-wire ground-truth
content who are willing to tune may still try it; the bench data above
gives them a realistic expectation.

### Why the rejection persists

The fundamental issue identified in `6c01965` is unchanged:

> butteraugli-loop converges the quant_field on the post-splines XYB so
> the measured residual-energy savings proxy falls below the 2×
> COST_BENEFIT_MARGIN at e8+.

Chunk 7 strengthens this: even when the proxy claims a win and the
candidate is admitted (1024-wide cases), the actual bytes go UP by
3-5%. The proxy is mis-calibrated at e8+; recalibrating it would need
to model the buttloop's residual re-quantization, which is materially
more compute than the detector's existing trial encode.

A future flip would need either:
1. Detector evolution: replace the L2 proxy with an actual
   one-iteration buttloop predictor (expensive — likely cancels the
   savings on real content).
2. Effort-axis split: keep auto-splines off at e8+ where buttloop runs,
   and add a separate per-image opt-in for the pre-buttloop effort
   levels (e5-e7) where the chunk-3 detector originally won.

Neither lands in chunk 7; default stays off.

## Reproducibility

```
cargo build --release --example auto_splines_chunk7_bench \
  --features 'std parallel butteraugli-loop'

CID22=/home/lilith/work/codec-corpus/CID22/CID22-512/training
CLIC=/home/lilith/work/codec-corpus/clic2025-1024
./target/release/examples/auto_splines_chunk7_bench \
  "photo:$CID22/207691.png" "photo:$CID22/2123337.png" \
  "photo:$CID22/klepas-Gentle-giants-of-the-sea-3.png" \
  "photo:$CID22/ularapi_Semarang_City_Logo.png" \
  "photo:$CID22/1001682.png" "photo:$CID22/1080721.png" \
  "photo:$CID22/1129482.png" "photo:$CID22/pexels-photo-1029599.png" \
